[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"PHP Functions: Definition, Calling, Arguments, Return Values, and Scope"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Functions are fundamental building blocks in PHP, allowing you to encapsulate reusable blocks of code. They promote modularity, readability, and maintainability. This tutorial covers defining functions, calling them, passing arguments, returning values, and understanding variable scope within functions."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining Functions"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In PHP, you define a function using the 'function' keyword, followed by the function name, parentheses '()', and curly braces '{}' to enclose the function's code block."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"<?php
function greet() {
  echo "Hello, world!";
}
?>"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The above code defines a function named 'greet'. This function, when called, will print "Hello, world!" to the output."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Calling Functions"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"To execute the code within a function, you need to call it by its name followed by parentheses '()'."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"<?php
greet(); // Calls the greet function
?>"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Calling 'greet()' will execute the code inside the 'greet' function, resulting in "Hello, world!" being printed."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Arguments"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Functions can accept arguments (also known as parameters), which are values passed into the function when it's called. These arguments can be used within the function's code."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"<?php
function greetUser($name) {
  echo "Hello, " . $name . "!";
}

greetUser("Alice"); // Calls the greetUser function with the argument "Alice"
?>"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'greetUser' accepts one argument, '$name'. When called with 'greetUser("Alice")', the function will print "Hello, Alice!"."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Return Values"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Functions can return a value using the 'return' statement. This value can then be used in the code that called the function."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"<?php
function add($num1, $num2) {
  return $num1 + $num2;
}

$sum = add(5, 3);
echo $sum; // Outputs 8
?>"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'add' function takes two arguments, '$num1' and '$num2', and returns their sum. The returned value is then assigned to the variable '$sum'."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Scope"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Scope refers to the visibility and accessibility of variables within different parts of your code. PHP has two main types of scope: global and local."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Global Scope:** Variables declared outside of any function have global scope and can be accessed from anywhere in the script, *except* directly inside functions without using the 'global' keyword."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Local Scope:** Variables declared inside a function have local scope and can only be accessed within that function."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"<?php
$globalVar = "Global Variable";

function myFunction() {
  $localVar = "Local Variable";
  global $globalVar; // Accessing the global variable inside the function
  echo $globalVar; // Outputs "Global Variable"
  echo $localVar; // Outputs "Local Variable"
}

myFunction();

echo $globalVar; // Outputs "Global Variable"
echo $localVar; // Results in an error: undefined variable
?>"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, '$globalVar' is a global variable, and '$localVar' is a local variable within 'myFunction'. To access '$globalVar' inside the function, the 'global' keyword is used. Trying to access '$localVar' outside the function results in an error because it's out of scope."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding functions, their arguments, return values, and scope is crucial for writing well-structured and maintainable PHP code. By using functions effectively, you can break down complex tasks into smaller, manageable units, making your code easier to understand, debug, and reuse."}]
